hi there steve goffin here today i'm  going to talk about active vocabulary as  opposed to passive vocabulary and active  vocabulary means output it means  speaking or writing in other words  the words become activated the words  that you accumulate i'm going to talk  about therefore active vocabulary i'm  also going to talk about  how you use tutors because that was a  question that came up  and  let me begin by saying first of all if  you like these videos please subscribe  give me a like if you want  but begin by saying that you know so  much of language learning is a matter of  attitude  getting the passive vocabulary activated  which means speaking or writing is a  matter of attitude  just like language learning if you are  interested in the language you're  confident that you can learn the  language you  you're confident that the system you're  using is going to help you  all of these  matters of attitude are more important  than which school you attend or  what book you use or anything else the  same with speaking  when i go to make these videos i don't  really plan ahead i am not afraid that  i'm going to run out of things to say  mind you i've been doing it for a while  now  i just go for it and i think when we  speak we have to have that attitude that  we're not afraid to make mistakes we're  not afraid to be criticized  we're just gonna go for it and whatever  comes out comes out  for example you will see  in a  couple of weeks a video that i did  with a person named mike who is a keen  learner of arabic and who lived in the  middle east and we spoke in arabic and i  hadn't spoken arabic for eight months i  had done a lot of input activity on link  saving words and phrases so i had no  idea how this would turn out but it  turned out  i miss some words i got some things  wrong but  it's amazing how  a significant investment in input  particularly where i save words and then  i save a phrase and i listen to the  phrase through the text to speech and  then i listen again to the same  content spoken in a natural voice it  just builds and builds and builds this  passive vocabulary this comprehension  which makes you comfortable and and  because then you you know you're going  to understand what's said and then  something comes out and you just have to  trust that something will come up and  there is no shortcut  you know it is only once you have a  significant opportunity or need to speak  and that you get to speak a lot that  your output  you know skills will significantly  improve if you are  living away from where the language is  spoken as is the case with my  arabic  i'm not going to get very good at  speaking arabic but i can build up my  potential through a lot of input  okay and as i've said before the passive  vocabulary is tremendously important  because comprehension is the core skill  if you understand  you will eventually be able to speak if  you don't understand you can't have much  of a conversation so now how do we  actually activate this passive  vocabulary first of all  you know let's start with the with  what is a word  because i've learned a number of  languages i'm comfortable with the fact  that we're just  basically dealing with terms here  in chinese most words consist of two  characters but you have to know both  characters in order to understand  the  you know the the compound  word the fujotsa which might even be  three characters so it doesn't matter to  me whether it's one two or three these  are vocabulary items that i have to  learn in persian  lots especially the verbs are compound  and so the individual components may not  have tremendous meaning but put together  as two or three  words in a way they become a word  you say equivalent to a word in english  and i just  learn them it doesn't matter to me  that's why i think for example this  discussion about whether you learn  words or collocations or phrasal verbs  it really doesn't matter you have to  learn these  call them lexical terms these  vocabulary terms you have to accumulate  them and so all of this stuff sucks into  your brain now how do you activate it  there are a number of strategies you can  write which i did for chinese because we  were asked to write if i'm not asked to  write i don't write so i just don't have  the discipline to start writing on my  own when i'm learning languages for fun  but when i was studying chinese at a  school i had to write and i think that  accelerated my  learning of chinese accelerated my  ability to speak the language so writing  is obviously a good thing to do because  you have time to think you can look  things up you can look up grammar rules  you can look up uh vocabulary and you  can you know structure something that's  a little more  you know dense and accurate and that's  training for  output you can look into if you want  your  saved vocabulary items and make sure you  use them so there's any number of things  that the reasons why writing is a good  place to start if you have the  discipline to do it which i don't then  the next thing is  if you are surrounded by native speakers  of course you can go talk to anyone and  that was my situation in japan at a very  early stage even though i didn't know  much japanese and i was continuing my  intensive input activities every  opportunity i had  whether it was uh you know in the  subway at the grocery store  even asking my colleagues at work to  speak to me in japanese i made every  effort to use the language because the  opportunity was there  but if i'm learning in vancouver or now  i'm down in palm springs i don't have  that opportunity so i continue with my  intensive input activities but at some  point i i start to use the services of  an online tutor  because  uh  okay  the disadvantage is that you can't do it  five hours a day but i might do it two  or three hours a week  and this person is obliged to listen to  you  and oblige to talk to you whereas you  know a random stranger may not have the  patience to talk to if you don't speak  the language very well  and so people sometimes say well how do  you prepare for a tutor  i don't do any preparing at a very early  stage in a language with a difficult  writing system like persian or arabic i  begin by  say taking our mini stories at link and  then reading them  with the tutor  during our our our lesson and then we  have a discussion about the mini story  so i have the vocabulary there from the  mini story i've listened to the mini  story ten times i've read it a couple of  times we read it again online and then  we talk about it so that's kind of a  lead into trying to say something in the  language but once i'm past that stage  basically we talk about anything and  everything and the good tutor is able to  ask you questions that get you to speak  ask you questions about things of  interest not just you know is it raining  in vancouver  and they keep you going so it's not my  i don't have to  you know i'm not stressed about  maintaining the conversation  the good tutor keeps the conversation  going and the lesson is strictly  conversation because  i can look up points of grammar  if i have a question of grammar  something that  despite having looked it up in google i  don't quite understand how the language  works i might ask the tutor in  the target language persian arabic and  the tutor will explain in my sessions  with a tutor we never ever speak english  we only speak the target language but  typically we just roam around different  subjects  the key thing is that i have built up a  level of comprehension that i can kind  of at least understand what the tutor is  saying  eventually  i struggled initially in korean but we  didn't have the many stories at that  time with the many stories it gets you  up to uh where you can basically  understand what they're saying even if  you're missing some words  and so then we have a conversation  and of course the the tutor has to be  encouraging you know i had a tutor i've  had tutors who give me a pained  expression when they don't understand  what i'm saying like huh  of course that's very very bad the tutor  has to be totally into encouraging you  so that you you you feel pumped up and  you want to go back to that tutor again  and then  if we speak for typically an hour the  tutor then while we're having our lesson  the tutor is maintaining a file in  google documents or something  of all the mistakes  that you make or all the words and  phrases that cost you difficulty and  then the tutor sends me typically either  it's if it's in if it's a link tutor i  get the report through link if it's not  if it's italki they send me a document  file of some kind and it has 10 or 15  phrases each phrase embodies  something that i had difficulty  expressing then the good tutors again  will record all of these phrases so then  i can go and prepare breakfast and i'm  listening to  these uh phrases  and it becomes and i've mentioned this  before it becomes a record because i  import all of these into link as lessons  so that i have a course at link which is  conversation reports so i have them say  in the case of arabic i can go back to  18 months ago and relive those  conversations that i had and it'll have  all the problem areas and it's kind of  fun to see that  some of the problems you had 18 months  ago you still have problems with and in  some cases these have become second  nature to you so  in these sessions with the tutor because  it's a protected area and i'm just  trying out whatever i can  use you know  i'm not deliberately reaching into my  passive vocabulary but as i speak i  surprise myself some stuff comes out and  uh and i don't worry about it much like  when i make this video here i haven't  planned it out ahead of time i have some  thoughts in my brain  similarly i go into these sessions with  my tutor with no real plan  except again as i say at the initial  stages when i'm so weak in the language  that i i can't really have a natural  fleet throwing  free-flowing conversation so we build it  around  you know content like the many stories  so i read and we discuss them but once  we're past that we're having an  unscripted natural conversation  it just comes out mistakes and all  and and you realize as you read these  conversation reports that you make the  same mistakes week after week after week  but gradually you improve  and similarly  it seems like you're searching for the  same words week after week after week as  i see that in my conversation reports  because our conversations tend to come  back to the same kinds of subjects and  it's the same words that i can't  remember but over time these words  gradually start to stick so i guess in  summary  i'll say that to activate you're not  going to activate all of your past  vocabulary because you need to have a  very large passive vocabulary in order  to understand what the other person is  saying but you will gradually activate  more of it more and more of it but it's  a slow process and ultimately to become  good at speaking in other words to have  a lot of active vocabulary you have to  speak a lot and if you're not in a  situation where you're surrounded by you  know native speakers where you engage  with them for hours at a time this is  very difficult to do if you're  you live in brazil or japan or germany  or somewhere and don't have an  opportunity to use the language a lot so  you have to be satisfied with continuing  your input activity  you know  engaging with tutors online and seeing  that very slow progression  and never ever worrying about it just  let it just let it come out  and you will see as i have seen that  that you gradually get better but the  progress is slow  but as long as the process is enjoyable  slow is okay  anyway i hope that's helpful  bye for now
 

